			<article>
				<p class="eyebrow">How-to guide</p>
				<h1>Review and commit an agent's changes</h1>
				<p class="lede">Read what an agent changed, compare its worktree with a branch, commit, and take the work through a pull request, beside the session that made the change.</p>
				<p>The right edge of the window has four buttons. They open the sessions, the files, <strong>Changes</strong>, and <strong>Pull request</strong> for the worktree on screen. <strong>Changes</strong> has a branch icon, and <strong>Pull request</strong> has the GitHub icon. Click the same button again to close the panel.</p>

				<h2 id="read-the-diff">Read the diff</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Select the session or worktree in the sidebar.</li>
					<li>Click the branch icon on the right edge to open <strong>Changes</strong>. You can also click the worktree's <code>+N −M</code> count in the sidebar.</li>
					<li>Click a file under <strong>Changes</strong> or <strong>Staged Changes</strong> to open its diff. Double-click to keep the diff open.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>To find any file in the worktree, press <kbd>⌘P</kbd>.</p>

				<h2 id="compare-with-a-branch">Compare with a branch</h2>
				<p>By default, <strong>Changes</strong> shows uncommitted changes. To see everything a worktree changed against a branch, as a pull request shows it:</p>
				<ol>
					<li>In the <strong>Changes</strong> header, click the scope switch, which reads <strong>Uncommitted</strong>.</li>
					<li>Pick a branch. The worktree's base branch is tagged <code>base</code>. Type in <strong>Search branches…</strong> to filter.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The switch now reads <code>vs &lt;branch&gt;</code>. The list shows the files changed from where the worktree left that branch to its latest commit. This view is read-only and has no commit box. Each worktree remembers its own choice. If the branch goes away, the view goes back to the base branch.</p>
				<p>To go back, click the switch and pick <strong>Uncommitted</strong>.</p>

				<h2 id="stage-discard-and-commit">Stage, discard, and commit</h2>
				<ol>
					<li>Hover over a file or a section header to show its buttons. The plus stages, the minus unstages, and the discard button discards.</li>
					<li>Type a message in the commit box.</li>
					<li>Press <kbd>⌘↩</kbd>, or click <strong>Commit</strong>.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>If nothing is staged, the button reads <strong>Commit All</strong> and commits every changed file. To commit only staged files, set Settings › <strong>Git &amp; GitHub</strong> › <strong>If nothing is staged</strong> to <strong>Staged only</strong>.</p>
				<p>The arrow next to the commit button has more choices: <strong>Commit &amp; Push</strong>, <strong>Commit, push and create PR</strong>, and <strong>Amend Last Commit</strong>.</p>
				<div class="callout warning">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Warning</span>Discarded changes can't be restored. Anywhere asks first, and says how many untracked files it will delete.</p>
				</div>
				<p>To have an agent write the commit message, turn on Settings › <strong>Git &amp; GitHub</strong> › <strong>Write commit messages with AI</strong>. A sparkle button then shows in the commit box.</p>

				<h2 id="push-and-open-a-pull-request">Push and open a pull request</h2>
				<p>Pull request features use the GitHub command line tool, <code>gh</code>. Install it and run <code>gh auth login</code>. Settings › <strong>Git &amp; GitHub</strong> › <strong>GitHub CLI</strong> shows whether it is ready.</p>
				<ol>
					<li>When nothing is left to commit, the commit box shows how many commits are ready. Click <strong>Push <var>n</var> commits</strong>.</li>
					<li>Click <strong>Create Pull Request</strong>.</li>
					<li>Fill in <strong>Title</strong> and <strong>Description</strong>, or wait for the agent to write them.</li>
					<li>Click <strong>Create PR</strong>. To open a draft, choose <strong>Create draft pull request</strong> from the button's menu.</li>
				</ol>

				<h2 id="use-the-pull-request-card">Use the pull request card</h2>
				<p>Click the GitHub icon on the right edge, or the <code>#123</code> chip in the <strong>Changes</strong> header, to open the <strong>Pull request</strong> panel. The icon takes the pull request's state colour, and a red dot shows when it needs something from you.</p>
				<p>The card's header shows the title, the number, and a verdict such as <strong>Ready to merge</strong>, <strong>Changes requested</strong>, or the number of checks that failed. The button with the external-link icon opens the pull request on GitHub. Below the header are three sections.</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Checks</strong> lists the checks. A failed check has <strong>Log</strong> to read its output and <strong>Send</strong> to hand it to the agent.</li>
					<li><strong>Review</strong> shows approvals, requested changes, and up to three open comments, each with <strong>Send</strong>.</li>
					<li><strong>Merge</strong> says what stands in the way, such as conflicts or checks that haven't passed.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>When there is more than one thing to fix, <strong>Send all</strong> sends them all to the agent at once.</p>

				<h3>Merge</h3>
				<ol>
					<li>Pick the merge method from the arrow on the merge button: <strong>Squash and merge</strong>, <strong>Create a merge commit</strong>, or <strong>Rebase and merge</strong>. Squash is the default.</li>
					<li>Click the merge button. It works only when the card says the pull request is ready.</li>
				</ol>
				<p><strong>Delete the worktree after merging</strong> is on by default. It keeps the branch. If the branch conflicts with the base, the merge button changes to <strong>Resolve with</strong> and the agent's name. For a draft, it changes to <strong>Ready for review</strong>.</p>

				<h3>Pull request links in a chat</h3>
				<p>A link to a GitHub pull request in a chat shows as a chip. If the pull request belongs to one of your worktrees, the chip shows its title, number, and status. Hover over the chip to open a card with <strong>Open in panel</strong> and <strong>Send to</strong> the agent.</p>
